The group of homomorphisms from the multiplicative group of non-zero complex numbers $\mathbb{C}^\ast$ to the additive group of integers $\mathbb{Z}$ is the trivial group. Formally:
\text{Hom}_{\text{grp}}(\mathbb{C}^\ast, \mathbb{Z}) = \{0\}

Every homomorphism $\phi: \mathbb{C}^\ast \to \mathbb{Z}$ must vanish on the torsion elements of $\mathbb{C}^\ast$ (roots of unity) because elements of finite order in $\mathbb{C}^\ast$ must map to torsion elements in any target group. The torsion subgroup of $\mathbb{C}^\ast$ is dense and generates the kernel of any such map when considering the quotient. The quotient $\mathbb{C}^\ast / \text{Tor}(\mathbb{C}^\ast)$ is isomorphic to the additive group of real numbers $\mathbb{R}$. Any homomorphism from $\mathbb{R}$ to $\mathbb{Z}$ is necessarily the zero map because $\mathbb{R}$ is a divisible group and $\mathbb{Z}$ is not. Consequently, the original homomorphism must be the trivial homomorphism.
